course: dictionary
lesson: 1924
title: Comfortable: more than cosy
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Many words have more than one meaning, and the sentence tells you which one to use. «Comfortable» is an adjective, so it describes a thing or a person. Its forms are comfortable, more comfortable and most comfortable.
explain: «The new bed was so comfortable that I fell asleep at once.» Here comfortable means pleasant to lie on, with no pain and no tightness. Now read «Sam felt comfortable with his new friends.» Here it means relaxed, calm and at ease. Same word, different meaning.
explain: Comfortable can also be about money or a contest. «The family had a comfortable income» means they had enough money to live well. «Our team had a comfortable lead» means the lead was big enough to feel safe. Ask yourself: what is being described, and which meaning fits?
explain: Feelings matter too. If someone makes you feel uncomfortable, you do not have to go along with it. You can say no and tell a trusted adult, such as a parent, a teacher or a school nurse.
fact: Comfortable is an adjective: comfortable, more comfortable, most comfortable.
fact: Comfortable can mean pleasant to use, sit in, wear or be in, without pain or being too hot, cold or tight.
fact: Comfortable can mean feeling relaxed, calm and at ease, not worried or shy.
fact: Comfortable can mean having enough money to live well without worry.
fact: In a contest, a comfortable lead is big enough to be safe. Useful phrases: «make yourself comfortable», «feel comfortable», «comfortable with», «a comfortable win».
exercise: «The new bed was so comfortable that I fell asleep at once.» What does comfortable mean here? | Noisy and full of bounce | Very dirty and old | *Pleasant to lie on, with no pain or tightness | Hard and cold like a stone
exercise: «Please make yourself comfortable while you wait.» What does this phrase ask you to do? | *Sit or relax in a way that feels at ease | Put on a heavy coat | Leave the room at once | Shout for help
exercise: Which sentence uses comfortable to mean a big, safe lead? | I feel comfortable with my cousin | This old armchair is comfortable to sit in | My shoes are comfortable for walking | *Our team had a comfortable lead at half time
test: «If someone makes you feel uncomfortable, what should you do?» | Keep it secret and say nothing | Laugh and forget it | Do what they ask, every time | *Say no and tell a trusted adult
test: Dad says, «The family had a comfortable income.» What does comfortable mean? | Had a big lead in a race | Felt very worried | Was dirty and cold | *Had enough money to live well
exam: «Ravi was comfortable with the idea of speaking first in class.» What does comfortable mean? | Pleasant to sit in | Far ahead of the others | *Relaxed and not worried | Having plenty of money
